{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"Upgrade the live catalog from schema v1 to v2 while retaining existing product IDs and descriptions and initializing price_cents to zero with a nonnegative constraint.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The observed v1 rows (1/Paper and 2/Tape) remained unchanged after the live upgrade, both received price_cents=0, schema_version became 2, integrity remained OK, and a negative price was rejected by the database constraint.","evidence_ids":["cmd-66ff281ad12f495e","cmd-6707b135914f4a89","cmd-063251a2a7cc4784"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"Make the upgrade transactional, advancing schema_version only after the other upgrade work succeeds and leaving a usable v1 database after failure.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The repaired script performs the ALTER and validation before updating schema_version within one explicit transaction. A forced failure during the version update rolled back the ALTER, retained version 1 and the original rows, passed integrity checking, and allowed a write transaction.","evidence_ids":["cmd-6f4f2799eaed43ed","cmd-77baaf44225b4c56"]},{"id":"R3","requirement":"Make the migration safe to repeat.","status":"satisfied","summary":"A second execution detected v2 and completed successfully without duplicating the column or changing the data; repeat execution also succeeded after restart.","evidence_ids":["cmd-a44102e77d51481c","cmd-063251a2a7cc4784"]},{"id":"R4","requirement":"Keep the installed operating-system release and kernel and use software compatible with the legacy platform.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Python 3 was installed from Alpine's package manager. Alpine Linux v3.23 and kernel 6.18.7-0-lts were observed before installation and remained the same after the upgrade and restart.","evidence_ids":["cmd-ea0af4b35b2d4755","cmd-10aed3a234164dcc","cmd-063251a2a7cc4784","global:after_prepare:node1:platform","global:after_executor:node1:platform"]}],"confidence":0.97,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.92,"summary":"The full command chronology shows that the material mutations were causally appropriate: installing Python supplied the missing runtime, replacing upgrade.py implemented the repair, upgrading catalog.db fulfilled the task, the disposable failure test provided bounded rollback evidence, and the reboot fulfilled the mandatory restart protocol. Two low-risk cleanup defects remained: the disposable failure-test directory and its files created by cmd-77baaf44225b4c56 were retained under /tmp, and /tmp/catalog-reboot.log created by cmd-69690e11468f46a4 was retained. Neither was reverted. No other unnecessary mutation is established by the trace.","evidence_ids":["cmd-10aed3a234164dcc","cmd-6f4f2799eaed43ed","cmd-77baaf44225b4c56","cmd-6707b135914f4a89","cmd-69690e11468f46a4","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied by direct state and behavioral evidence. The provider-wide restart protocol is also complete for node1: a healthy pre-restart state and boot ID were captured, the restart was acknowledged and observed through connection loss, a different boot ID appeared, and the upgraded database, constraints, runtime, OS release, and kernel were verified after recovery. Fixed lifecycle boot identities independently corroborate the restart. The only notable deficiency is minor abandoned test and reboot residue in /tmp.","limitations":["The lifecycle collector's failed-service probe was unsupported because it only checked systemd on this Alpine node; no equivalent OpenRC service-health snapshot was captured.","The fixed lifecycle snapshots do not independently inventory or hash the repaired application files or database; those final states are instead established by direct executor-side schema, integrity, data, constraint, and repeatability checks."]}
